❮ Scanner Methods

### Code Examples

```
Example
Read comma separated items:
// Create a scanner object
Scanner myObj = new Scanner("Item 1,Item 2,Item 3");

// Change delimiter
myObj.useDelimiter(",");

// Read the contents of the scanner
while (myObj.hasNext()) {
  System.out.println(myObj.next());
}
```

## Tutorials

❮ Scanner Methods

TheuseLocale()method changes the locale used by the scanner. The locale determines how numbers are interpreted by deciding how digits are grouped, which character serves as a decimal point, etc.

TheuseLocale()method requires aLocaleobject as an argument.Localeobjects represent a language or country and they are used by a variety of Java classes to handle formatting and interpreting data.

The easiest way to get aLocaleobject is by using one of the objects provided by attributes of theLocaleclass.

A list of available language and country attributes is shown below.

If the country or language you need is not in the list then you can create a newLocaleobject using a language code and an optional country code. Most codes are two or three characters long and each code represents a language or a country.

❮ Scanner Methods

### Code Examples

```
Example
Read numbers from a different locale:
// Create a scanner object
Scanner myObj = new Scanner("1.500.000");

// Change delimiter
myObj.useLocale(new Locale("es"));

// Read and display the number
System.out.println(myObj.nextInt());
```

## Tutorials

❮ Scanner Methods

TheuseRadix()method changes the radix used by the scanner. The radix specifies how many different symbols can be used to represent each digit in a number. For example, a radix of 16 would allow the symbols 0 to 9 and A to F to be used as digits.

❮ Scanner Methods

### Code Examples

```
Example
Read a hexadecimal number:
// Create a scanner object
Scanner myObj = new Scanner("FFAD01");

// Change radix
myObj.useRadix(16);

// Read and display the number
System.out.println(myObj.nextInt());
```

